-- title:
--   Kronecker congruence: relations vanish on both branches
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$, and an intermediate field $K$ of $\mathbb Q \subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}$; write $A_0 = A \cap K$ for the subring `coeffSubring A K` of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ cut out as the intersection of $A$ with $K$. Let $s$ be a polynomial in two variables (indexed by `Fin 2`) with coefficients in $A_0$, and assume that `modularEval` of $s$ at level $1\cdot q$ vanishes, that is, substituting the Laurent series `jqModC` (the $\mathsf q$-expansion $\mathsf q^{-1}\cdot(\text{power series})$ of $j$) for the variable $0$ and `jqNModC` at $N = 1\cdot q$ (the same series with $\mathsf q$ replaced by $\mathsf q^{q}$) for the variable $1$, with coefficients sent to constant Laurent series, gives $0$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$. The conclusion is a conjunction: reducing the coefficients of $s$ by $\mathrm{red}$ restricted to $A_0$ and evaluating in $k[X]$ gives $\bar s(X, X^{q}) = 0$ and also $\bar s(X^{q}, X) = 0$.
--
--   This is Kronecker's congruence for the modular equation in the form needed here: the reduction modulo $q$ of any plane relation satisfied by $(j(\mathsf q), j(\mathsf q^{q}))$ vanishes on both components $X_1 = X_0^{q}$ and $X_0 = X_1^{q}$ of the special fibre of $X_0(q)$. It feeds the analysis of the node on that special fibre, being used to prove that the ideal generated by a uniformizer together with the two branches in the localized ring at a node point is prime.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_NodeLocalized_eval2_branch_eq_zero_of_modularEval_eq_zero.lean

